From: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>
Subject: [PATCH] ath11k: skip nss pdev deinit for an already destroyed radio

ath11k_nss_pdev_deinit() takes its radio from ab->pdevs[radio_id].ar and
dereferences it unchecked, first through ath11k_nss_peer_stats_disable() and
then for the deinit message itself. ath11k_mac_destroy() NULLs that pointer for
every radio, and it sits on the ath11k_core_start() error path - the one a
crash recovery takes when the firmware never answers with the unified ready
event.

ath11k_core_reconfigure_on_crash() restores ab->nss.enabled from the module
parameter before it calls ath11k_core_qmi_firmware_ready(), and the failure
path back out does not clear it again. A recovery that dies there therefore
leaves the driver with offload still enabled and no radios at all, so the
teardown at the top of the next reconfigure walks straight into the NULL.

The first dereference reached is ar->ab in ath11k_nss_stats_cfg(), which every
offload build takes because ath11k_core_pre_init() sets nss.stats_enabled
alongside nss.enabled. ab is the first member of struct ath11k, which is why
the fault address is exactly 0.

A radio that has already been freed has nothing left to deinit - its NSS
interface number and context lived in the ath11k that ath11k_mac_destroy()
released - so return early and let the teardown loop account for it.

Signed-off-by: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>

diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/nss.c b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/nss.c
index 4235a84..3f4cc12 100644
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/nss.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/nss.c
@@ -3484,6 +3484,12 @@
 	int dyn_if_type;
 	int ret;
 
+	/* A recovery that fails after ath11k_mac_destroy() leaves the radios
+	 * freed but nss.enabled set, so the next teardown lands here with none.
+	 */
+	if (!ar)
+		return -ENODEV;
+
 	ath11k_dbg(ab, ATH11K_DBG_NSS, "NSS pdev %d deinit\n", radio_id);
 	dyn_if_type = ath11k_nss_get_dynamic_interface_type(ab);
 
